    "body": "## Overview\n\nThe fa2 NVFP4 KV path refuses head_size 64 because FlashInfer's fa2 prefill misreads the cache at that size. [FlashInfer #6024](https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/pull/6024) fixes the kernel; this drops the two refusals and runs the existing NVFP4 decode test at head_size 64 as well. That PR is merged and ships in the next FlashInfer release. On the current 0.7.2.post1 pin, head_size 64 models would still get wrong attention and the new test cases fail, so this should land with or after the bump to that release.\n\n## Claims\n\n- With FlashInfer PR 6024, fa2 reads an NVFP4 cache at head_size 64 as accurately as at 128 and 256: its prefill output matches SDPA over the dequantized cache to 0.002–0.003 relative error, on random inputs and on q/k/v captured from two head_size 64 models.\n- `test_flashinfer_nvfp4_decode_correctness` at head_size 64 fails on FlashInfer 0.7.2 and passes with PR 6024.\n- Other head sizes and KV cache dtypes are unchanged.\n\n## Validation\n\nsm_80, main (`c723f6d3ee`) plus this commit. \"0.7.2\" is the pinned FlashInfer release; \"0.7.2 + 6024\" is the same release with PR 6024's `prefill.cuh` change applied.\n\n```text\npytest tests/v1/attention/test_attention_backends.py -k test_flashinfer_nvfp4_decode_correctness\n    # 0.7.2: 2 failed (head_size 64, q_len 4), 6 passed; 0.7.2 + 6024: 8 passed\npytest tests/kernels/attention/test_flashinfer.py -k nvfp4    # 4 passed (both)\npytest tests/kernels/attention/test_attention_selector.py    # 87 passed, 22 skipped, 2 failed\n```\n\nThe two selector failures (`test_hopper_mm_prefix_selects_triton_flash_attn[None-True]` and `[4-True]`) fail the same way on main.\n\nfa2 NVFP4 prefill output against SDPA over the cache read back with `nvfp4_kv_dequantize_paged` (relative L2 error, random inputs, sequence lengths 40, 300 and 1027; a local script, not added here):\n\n| q / kv heads, head_size | 0.7.2 | 0.7.2 + 6024 |\n|---|---|---|\n| 32 / 8, 64 | 0.0746 | 0.0034 |\n| 14 / 2, 64 | 0.0741 | 0.0033 |\n| 16 / 8, 128 | 0.0033 | 0.0033 |\n| 24 / 4, 256 | 0.0033 | 0.0033 |\n\nThe same check on q/k/v captured from the first GSM8K 5-shot prompt gives, over all layers, at most 0.2636 on 0.7.2 and 0.0019 with PR 6024 for Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct (641 tokens, 16 layers), and 0.1971 and 0.0018 for Qwen2-0.5B-Instruct (726 tokens, 24 layers).\n\nGSM8K (1319 questions, 5-shot, greedy; prompts and scoring from `tests/evals/gsm8k`; offline `LLM` with the FLASHINFER backend):\n\n| Model (head_size) | BF16 KV | NVFP4 KV, 0.7.2 | NVFP4 KV, 0.7.2 + 6024 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct (64) | 0.319 | 0.127 | 0.249 |\n| Qwen2-0.5B-Instruct (64) | 0.372 | 0.016 | 0.016 |\n| Qwen3-0.6B (128) | 0.399 | 0.177 | 0.180 |\n\nThe NVFP4 cache costs these small models a lot of accuracy at head_size 128 too. On Qwen2-0.5B it costs nearly everything with or without the fix: on the captured inputs the kernel matches the dequantized reference in every layer, while quantization alone moves the first layer's attention output by 0.999 relative to BF16.\n\n## Details\n\nAt head_size 64, fa2 prefill pairs a 128-row query tile with a 64-row KV tile. The zero-fill `cp.async` that loads the V scale factors writes zeros even when its predicate is off, so the lanes past the scale-factor bytes overwrite the first two rows of the query tile in shared memory. PR 6024 stops those lanes; head_size 128 and 256 give the same results with and without it.\n\nOn the vLLM side, `FlashInferBackend.supports_combination` rejected NVFP4 at head_size 64 on SM8x, SM90 and SM12x, and the metadata builder raised for it; both go, and the sink refusal stays. SM12x decodes NVFP4 with XQA, which vLLM already routes for head sizes 16–256 in multiples of 16. Only sm_80 was run here; the SM90 fa2 path and SM12x were not.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [x] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>",
